
<% content_for :title, "When You Don't Trust Your Mood | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="mood-check-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="mood-check-hero">

    <picture class="mood-check-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_when_you_dont_trust_your_mood_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_when_you_dont_trust_your_mood_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="mood-check-hero-content">

      <p class="mood-check-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / WHEN YOU DON'T TRUST YOUR MOOD
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>MOOD CHECK</span>
        <span>BEFORE</span>
        <span>YOU WRECK.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="mood-check-intro">
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO BELIEVE
        EVERYTHING YOUR BRAIN TELLS YOU.
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO DISMISS IT EITHER.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / CHECK IN -->
  <section class="mood-check-section mood-check-pink">
    <div class="mood-check-inner">

      <p class="mood-check-number">01 / CHECK IN</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT FEELS<br>
        DIFFERENT?
      </h2>

      <div class="mood-check-copy">

        <p>
          There's a particular kind
          of frustration that comes
          with questioning your
          own judgment.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're the one having
          the thoughts, experiencing
          the feelings, and making
          the decisions. But you're
          also supposed to figure
          out whether the person
          doing all that thinking
          is seeing things clearly.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's a strange position
          to find yourself in.
          Especially when everything
          makes perfect sense
          from the inside.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you've become
          convinced that a decision
          is brilliant. Maybe
          something feels hopeless.
          Maybe you're furious
          with someone and
          can't tell whether
          you're overreacting.
        </p>

        <p>
          Or maybe there's
          nothing dramatic happening.
          You just have that
          uncomfortable suspicion
          that your perspective
          isn't quite what
          it usually is.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          immediately decide
          whether you're right,
          wrong, stable, or
          experiencing symptoms.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start by admitting
          that you're not
          entirely sure.
        </p>

        <p>
          Uncertainty isn't
          the same as being
          incapable of making
          decisions. It's
          information worth
          taking into account.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="mood-check-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT AM I SO CERTAIN
          ABOUT RIGHT NOW?
          AND WHAT IF I'M NOT
          SEEING THE WHOLE PICTURE?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / LOOK FOR CHANGES -->
  <section class="mood-check-section mood-check-black">
    <div class="mood-check-inner">

      <p class="mood-check-number">02 / QUESTION THE CONCLUSION</p>

      <h2>
        CHECK YOUR<br>
        LOGIC.
      </h2>

      <div class="mood-check-copy">

        <p>
          You can build a convincing
          argument for almost anything
          when you've already decided
          what you want the answer
          to be.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's the problem with
          evaluating your own judgment.
          You're using the same
          mind that reached the
          conclusion to decide
          whether the conclusion
          makes sense.
        </p>

        <p>
          So don't just ask
          whether your reasoning
          sounds good. Look at
          what you're actually
          basing it on.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          prove yourself wrong.
          You just need to
          make room for
          another possibility.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="mood-check-grid">

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>THE FACTS.</h3>

          <p>
            What do you actually
            know about the situation?
          </p>

          <p>
            What happened,
            what was said,
            and what information
            can you verify?
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the
            circumstances themselves,
            without adding your
            interpretation yet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>THE STORY.</h3>

          <p>
            What have you
            concluded from
            those facts?
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you assuming
            you know someone's
            intentions? Predicting
            an outcome? Treating
            a possibility
            like a certainty?
          </p>

          <p>
            Your interpretation
            might be correct.
            It still isn't
            the same thing
            as a fact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>THE CERTAINTY.</h3>

          <p>
            How convinced are
            you that you're right?
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you willing
            to consider another
            explanation, or
            have you already
            decided that anyone
            who disagrees
            is wrong?
          </p>

          <p>
            Confidence can
            be useful. It
            isn't evidence
            by itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>THE EMOTION.</h3>

          <p>
            What are you feeling,
            and how might that
            feeling be influencing
            your conclusions?
          </p>

          <p>
            Anger can make
            everything seem
            deliberate. Depression
            can make problems
            appear permanent.
            Excitement can make
            risks seem irrelevant.
          </p>

          <p>
            The feeling is
            real. The conclusion
            deserves a separate look.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>THE CONTRADICTIONS.</h3>

          <p>
            Is there anything
            that doesn't fit
            your interpretation?
          </p>

          <p>
            Have you dismissed
            information because
            it contradicts what
            you already believe?
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have
            to change your
            mind immediately.
            Just stop pretending
            conflicting evidence
            doesn't exist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>THE STAKES.</h3>

          <p>
            What happens if
            you're wrong?
          </p>

          <p>
            Would you lose
            some time, waste
            a little money,
            damage a relationship,
            or put your
            safety at risk?
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger the
            consequences, the
            more important
            another perspective
            becomes.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR BASELINE -->
  <section class="mood-check-section mood-check-yellow">
    <div class="mood-check-inner">

      <p class="mood-check-number">03 / YOUR PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2>
        NORMAL<br>
        FOR YOU.
      </h2>

      <div class="mood-check-copy">

        <p>
          You've spent enough
          time learning about
          bipolar disorder
          to know what your
          episodes can look like.
        </p>

        <p>
          But knowing the
          symptoms doesn't
          always tell you
          what to believe
          when you're in
          the middle of
          an experience.
        </p>

        <p>
          Think about decisions
          you've made while
          feeling absolutely
          certain of yourself.
          Some worked out.
          Others made considerably
          more sense at the time
          than they did afterward.
        </p>

        <p>
          You've also experienced
          depression convincing you
          that circumstances were
          hopeless, only to see
          things differently
          when your mood changed.
        </p>

        <p>
          That doesn't mean
          your experiences
          weren't real or
          that your concerns
          didn't matter.
        </p>

        <p>
          It means your perspective
          isn't completely independent
          of how you're feeling.
        </p>

        <p>
          And neither is
          anybody else's.
          You just have
          additional reasons
          to pay attention
          when your judgment
          feels unfamiliar.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider how you
          might approach this
          situation when you're
          feeling reasonably steady.
        </p>

        <p>
          Would you make
          the same decision?
          Reach the same conclusion?
          Feel the same urgency?
        </p>

        <p>
          You can't know
          for certain. But
          asking is useful.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="mood-check-callout">
        <p>
          IF I WERE FEELING STEADY,
          WOULD I SEE THIS THE SAME WAY?
          WHAT HAS PAST EXPERIENCE
          TAUGHT ME ABOUT MY JUDGMENT?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / ANOTHER PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="mood-check-section mood-check-purple">
    <div class="mood-check-inner">

      <p class="mood-check-number">04 / ANOTHER PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2>
        BORROW A<br>
        SECOND OPINION.
      </h2>

      <div class="mood-check-copy">

        <p>
          There's nothing particularly
          comfortable about asking
          someone else to evaluate
          a decision you're already
          convinced is reasonable.
        </p>

      
        <p>
          And somebody disagreeing
          with you doesn't
          automatically mean
          your judgment is impaired.
        </p>

        <p>
          But refusing to
          consider another perspective
          because you're certain
          you're right isn't
          particularly useful either.
        </p>

        <p>
          Choose someone who
          respects your autonomy
          and can be honest
          with you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask them to challenge
          the reasoning, not
          simply validate
          your conclusion.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can listen,
          disagree, and still
          benefit from the
          conversation.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="mood-check-grid">

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>WHO DO YOU TRUST?</h3>

          <p>
            Someone who knows
            you well enough
            to recognize when
            your behavior
            is unusual.
          </p>

          <p>
            Someone who won't
            automatically assume
            you're having an
            episode because
            you're upset
            or excited.
          </p>

          <p>
            Someone willing
            to tell you
            something you
            might not want
            to hear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your therapist can help
            when you're concerned
            about your mood
            or perceptions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>WHAT CAN YOU ASK?</h3>

          <p>
            "I'm having trouble
            figuring out whether
            I'm seeing this clearly.
            Can I run something
            by you?"
          </p>

          <p>
            "Does my reasoning
            make sense, or
            am I overlooking
            something important?"
          </p>

          <p>
            "If you disagree
            with me, can
            you explain why?
            I want to understand
            what I'm missing."
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask for an honest
            perspective, not
            automatic agreement
            or permission.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / PRESS PAUSE -->
  <section class="mood-check-section mood-check-black">
    <div class="mood-check-inner">

      <p class="mood-check-number">05 / THE DECISION</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT CAN<br>
        FUCKING WAIT?
      </h2>

      <div class="mood-check-copy">

        <p>
          Sometimes you can't
          settle the question
          of whether your mood
          is affecting your
          judgment.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can think through
          the situation, examine
          the evidence, and ask
          someone for perspective,
          but still feel uncertain.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's when the
          practical question
          becomes more important
          than the explanation.
        </p>

        <p>
          What actually needs
          to happen right now?
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're considering
          a major purchase,
          a financial commitment,
          a confrontation, or
          a dramatic change,
          ask whether waiting
          would create a
          genuine problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          decide whether the
          idea is good or bad.
          You can decide
          you're not ready
          to act on it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Write it down.
          Preserve the information.
          Give yourself an
          opportunity to reconsider
          when you have more
          perspective.
        </p>

        <p>
          If your mood is
          significantly unstable,
          important decisions may
          need to wait until
          you've discussed them
          with your treatment team.
        </p>

        <p>
          And if something is
          genuinely urgent,
          including a medical
          or safety concern,
          get the appropriate
          help now.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have
          to solve the
          uncertainty before
          making a reasonable
          choice about what
          happens next.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="mood-check-callout">
        <p>
          I DON'T HAVE TO DECIDE
          WHETHER I'M RIGHT TODAY.
          I CAN DECIDE WHAT'S SAFE,
          NECESSARY, AND REASONABLE
          TO DO NEXT.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / YOUR SUPPORT PLAN -->
  <section class="mood-check-section mood-check-pink">
    <div class="mood-check-inner">

      <p class="mood-check-number">06 / YOUR SUPPORT PLAN</p>

      <h2>
        KNOW YOUR<br>
        NEXT MOVE.
      </h2>

      <div class="mood-check-copy">

        <p>
          Not trusting your mood
          doesn't mean you can't
          trust yourself at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can trust yourself
          to recognize when
          something deserves
          a second look.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can trust yourself
          to take precautions,
          ask questions, and
          involve people who
          can help you
          evaluate the situation.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need
          absolute certainty
          about what's happening
          before you make
          a responsible decision.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the decision
          is to wait. Sometimes
          it's to gather more
          information. Sometimes
          it's to call your psych.
        </p>

        <p>
          And sometimes, after
          considering everything,
          you discover that
          your original judgment
          was perfectly reasonable.
        </p>

        <p>
          The point isn't
          to automatically
          distrust yourself.
          It's to recognize
          when certainty
          deserves a little
          more scrutiny.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can have bipolar
          disorder and still
          know yourself.
          You can question
          a conclusion without
          dismissing your experience.
        </p>

        <p>
          And you can be
          uncertain about
          your mood without
          giving up control
          over your life.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="mood-check-grid">

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>WHEN I QUESTION MY JUDGMENT.</h3>

          <p>
            Identify the actual
            decision or belief
            I'm questioning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Separate what I
            know from what
            I'm assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider what
            evidence I might
            be overlooking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask whether my
            current mood could
            be influencing
            my perspective.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't confuse
            feeling certain
            with being right.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mood-check-box">
          <h3>WHO CAN HELP ME THINK?</h3>

          <p>
            A trusted person
            who can offer
            an honest perspective.
          </p>

          <p>
            Someone who respects
            my independence
            without automatically
            agreeing with me.
          </p>

          <p>
            My therapist or
            psychiatrist when
            I'm concerned that
            my mood is affecting
            my judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            I can ask for
            specific observations
            rather than letting
            someone else decide
            what my experience means.
          </p>
        </div>

        

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
